This is a starting-point skeleton, not a package ready for an official
Debian upload (that step is a human/social process, not agent work). What
it gets a packager past, and what it deliberately leaves open:

Builds via dh + CMake/Ninja, reusing the same cmake/{Rust,Backends,
Packaging}.cmake the CPack .deb (Track C1, already lintian-clean) is built
from — debian/rules only overrides the four dh_auto_* steps CMake's
buildsystem needs (configure with -G Ninja, build, install per-component to
skip the demo module under src/, clean). It has been built end-to-end with
`dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b` on the CI-image dependency set
(ci/install-deps.sh) plus `debhelper`, which that list does not otherwise
need (Track C1's CPack path has no dh dependency); not added to
ci/install-deps.sh here since nothing in CI runs this path yet.

**The open problem this does NOT solve: Rust crate vendoring.**
`cargo build` pulls ~140 crates (rust/Cargo.lock) from crates.io. An actual
Debian archive build happens on a buildd with no network access, so this
only builds today because whichever machine runs it already has those
crates in `~/.cargo/registry` from earlier `cargo build`/`cargo test` runs —
convenient for local iteration, but not what an official build allows, and
not proof this would survive one. Two real paths forward, same choice
docs/packaging/copyright already flags for Track C2 (which needs it decided
for exactly the flip side of this problem — DEP-5 has no `Files:` pattern
for sources that are not vendored):
  (a) `cargo vendor` the full dependency graph into the source package (an
      `orig.tar` that ships ~140 crates' source, checked with
      `dh-cargo`/`cargo-checksum.json`) — the common approach for Rust
      leaf packages already in Debian; or
  (b) `debcargo`-generate a `librust-*-dev` binary package per dependency
      and depend on them — the "every crate is its own Debian source
      package" approach official archive policy prefers, and a large,
      ongoing undertaking against a ~140-crate graph that changes with
      every `cargo update`.
Neither is attempted here — this skeleton documents the gap rather than
guessing at (a) or (b), per the roadmap text's own instruction not to
pretend it away. Whoever picks this up should decide (a) vs (b) before
`debian/rules` can be trusted to build from a bare `.dsc` rather than from a
working tree with a warm cargo cache.

**Also not verified — no network for real:** `debian/watch` follows the
usual GitHub-tags convention (`v0.1.0`, `v0.2.0` per the existing tags), but
`uscan`/`devscripts` are not installed on this VM, so it has not been run
against the real GitHub tags page. `debian/source/format` is `3.0 (quilt)`
with an empty patch queue (`debian/patches/` does not exist yet) — there is
nothing to patch upstream for today.

`debian/changelog` and `debian/copyright` are symlinks to
`docs/packaging/changelog`/`docs/packaging/copyright` rather than separate
copies: those files are already in the right formats (the changelog is
genuine Debian changelog syntax; the copyright is DEP-5) and already kept
current — the changelog by hand alongside `CMakeLists.txt`'s `VERSION`, the
copyright by Track C2's `tools/generate-debian-copyright.py` — so this
avoids a second, driftable copy of either. `dh_auto_test` is a deliberate
no-op (see the comment in debian/rules): the same Rust test suite runs
through `ci/checks.sh`/CTest against this exact source, and re-running it
here would need the same crates.io access the install step above already
cannot promise on a real buildd.
